  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nTonight through next Tuesday...High pressure aloft and at the \nsurface will remain in place across the region through the forecast \nperiod. Therefore, expect a continuation of hot temperatures and \nhumid conditions into early next week.  Highs each afternoon will \nwarm into the middle and upper 90s. This, as dew points mix each day \nin the low and middle 70s, will continue to yield afternoon heat \nindex values above the century mark.  This prolonged period of \ndangerous heat stress is expected, with a \"Heat Advisory\" at a \nminimum needed for portions of, if not all of, the forecast area \neach day.\n\nAn isolated shower or storm will be possible across mainly southern \nportions of the CWA, in the vicinity of the Highway 84 corridor \ntoday.  A slug of moisture, associated with a disturbance, will lift \nnorth from the Gulf of America and into the region heading into \nLabor Day weekend.  Better rain chances will likewise spread \nnorthward into the CWA Thursday into the weekend. Diurnally driven \nshowers and storms will remain possible through the weekend into \nearly next week.  While storms are currently expected to remain \nbelow severe limits, some gusty winds could accompany the most \nintense storms. Frequent lightning and heavy downpours will \nbe possible with any storms that develop. /19/",
